WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.933
Model families.

2
00:00:02.233 --> 00:00:06.244
These are the names that anchor the which model would you use conversation.

3
00:00:06.244 --> 00:00:07.100
Let's meet them.

4
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:09.767
This is OpenAI's line.

5
00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:11.833
It used to come in two flavors.

6
00:00:12.133 --> 00:00:15.936
Since GPT-5 in twenty twenty-five it is one family

7
00:00:15.936 --> 00:00:20.195
with mini and nano tiers and a reasoning-effort setting,

8
00:00:20.195 --> 00:00:25.367
replacing the old split between GPT-4o chat models and the o-series.

9
00:00:25.667 --> 00:00:29.870
The canonical routing example: send simple requests to the mini tier,

10
00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.733
and hard ones to the full model at high effort.

11
00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:36.333
Anthropic's Claude family comes in tiers.

12
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:38.130
Haiku is fast and cheap.

13
00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.000
Sonnet is the balanced middle.

14
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.550
Then the top tier.

15
00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:47.733
Claude is known for long context, agentic tool use, computer use, and for stewarding MCP.

16
00:00:48.933 --> 00:00:51.567
Gemini is Google's multimodal family.

17
00:00:51.867 --> 00:00:55.717
Its calling card is the longest mainstream context windows,

18
00:00:55.717 --> 00:00:59.567
a million tokens and more, plus native video understanding.

19
00:00:59.867 --> 00:01:02.500
On Google Cloud you reach it through Vertex AI.

20
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:07.067
DeepSeek is the Chinese lab that shook 2025.

21
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:10.184
V3 is an efficient mixture of experts,

22
00:01:10.184 --> 00:01:16.633
and R1 is an open reasoning model trained with GRPO, at a fraction of the assumed cost.

23
00:01:16.933 --> 00:01:20.933
The lesson: frontier-adjacent capability can be cheap, and open.

24
00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:24.567
Phi is Microsoft's small-model family.

25
00:01:24.867 --> 00:01:28.567
Its thesis is that textbook-quality training data beats sheer size.

26
00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:33.667
That makes Phi-4-class models strong candidates for on-device and edge use.

27
00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:37.300
Command R is Cohere's enterprise line.

28
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.406
It is purpose-tuned for RAG, with built-in citation grounding,

29
00:01:41.406 --> 00:01:43.800
so answers point back to their sources.

30
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:47.500
Around it sit strong multilingual embeddings and rerankers.

31
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:52.100
Jamba is AI21's hybrid Mamba-transformer.

32
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.467
It mixes state space layers with attention, so you get huge context at lower memory.

33
00:01:57.767 --> 00:02:00.933
It's the flagship proof that these hybrids ship in production.

34
00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:04.700
Two names to recognize, and no more than that.

35
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:10.173
Falcon is the open-weight line from TII, an early open LLM leader.

36
00:02:10.173 --> 00:02:15.033
Grok is from xAI, with open-weight releases of older versions.

37
00:02:15.333 --> 00:02:16.767
Just know the maker of each.

38
00:02:17.967 --> 00:02:18.868
Let's recap.

39
00:02:18.868 --> 00:02:23.900
OpenAI is one GPT family with tiers and a reasoning-effort setting.

40
00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.833
Claude comes in tiers, and Gemini has the long context.

41
00:02:28.133 --> 00:02:31.333
DeepSeek proved capability can be cheap and open.

42
00:02:31.633 --> 00:02:35.467
Phi, Jamba, Command R, Falcon and Grok each fill a niche.
